.alias fire_level        $4C5B    ;level the current part is played at, 0-$1F: twice the part's level plus diff_adder, with $20 and up folded into $10-$1F. each part's setup writes it, the enemy gun code of page 1 indexes its shot tables with it and fires when a 5 bit random number is below it. the probot part steps it up every 32 frames once its quota is met or 100 frames have passed. In ESB: the 5 bit random number compared with it does not decide firing: in gun_aim_error on page 1 a number below the level gives the new shot a random aim error, otherwise the shot homes on the player exactly. whether an enemy fires is decided by the entry the level picks in gun_fire_tab (TIE fighters, AT-ATs and AT-STs, the level held at 16 and for the TIE fighters halved during 16 frames of every 64) or in probot_fire_tab (probots, held at 15): the entry gives the time the enemy waits before it asks again, the value a random byte has to exceed and the highest gun record it may take
.alias w1_pace           $4C5D    ;wave 1: starts as fire_level and steps up every 16 frames (wave1_level_up). Page 2 (P2_91A8) indexes its table of random delays with it, capped at 8, shorter as it rises.
